flows/<device>/flows.csv
    The flow records Woodshed stored from each exporter in the window (flow
    time: a record is in when its start-to-end overlaps the window), one row
    per record, CSV with a header row (UTF-8, RFC 4180, CRLF). Columns:
        start,end,event_time,src,src_port,dst,dst_port,proto,tcp_flags,packets,bytes,sampling,packets_scaled,bytes_scaled,in_if,in_if_name,out_if,out_if_name,domain,fw_event,nat_event,post_nat_src,post_nat_src_port,post_nat_dst,post_nat_dst_port,initiator_bytes,responder_bytes,initiator_packets,responder_packets,rev_bytes,rev_packets,rule_id,tos,src_as,dst_as,next_hop,time_source,status,received
    Times are UTC, RFC 3339 with milliseconds. An empty cell means the record
    didn't carry that field. proto is the protocol number, tcp_flags the set
    flags as letters (S A F R P U E C), fw_event and nat_event by name.
    sampling is 1 (unsampled), N (1 in N) or 0 (unknown); the *_scaled
    columns multiply by it and are empty when it's unknown. Enterprise and
    other fields without a column aren't in the CSV; the IPFIX file has them.

flows/<device>/flows.ipfix
    The same records as an IPFIX file (RFC 5655), for flow tools: one
    observation domain per original domain, templates carrying every field
    the records had, enterprise fields included; NetFlow v5 and v9 records
    are written as their IPFIX fields. Woodshed wrote it from stored records
    (an options record in each domain says so); it wasn't captured off the
    wire. Not carried: the decode status, the exporter's source port and the
    original template IDs.

flows/<device>/raw.txt
    Only when there are any: flow data stored undecoded in the window, data
    whose template never arrived (no_template) and malformed packets, one per
    line: <received> <status> v<version> domain=<id> template=<id> <base64>.

manifest.json
    What was exported: the request (devices with their names and groups, the
    range and any filters), each log file's line count and SHA-256, and "gaps":
    periods in the range when Woodshed wasn't running or a listener was down,
    so "the device sent nothing" can be told apart from "Woodshed wasn't
    listening". "gaps_note" says how the gaps are worked out.
    A bundle's manifest also has "at", "before", "after" and "summary": the
    numbers above, per device, as JSON ("summary_note" says where they come
    from).

woodshed-events.log
    Woodshed's own events in the range: starts and stops, listener bind errors,
    file rotations and deletions, dropped messages, paused writes.

To check a file: sha256sum flows/<device>/flows.csv (or on Windows:
certutil -hashfile <file> SHA256) and compare with manifest.json.
